% EMNLP 2026 Main - ARR May 2026
% Generational Depth Contamination
\documentclass[11pt]{article}
\usepackage[review]{acl}
\usepackage{times}
\usepackage{latexsym}
\usepackage[T1]{fontenc}
\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}
\usepackage{microtype}
\usepackage{graphicx}
\usepackage{amsmath}
\usepackage{amssymb}
\usepackage{booktabs}
\usepackage{multirow}
\usepackage{enumitem}
\usepackage{subcaption}
\usepackage{pifont}
\newcommand{\cmark}{\ding{51}}
\newcommand{\xmark}{\ding{55}}
\usepackage{placeins}

\newcommand{\Kstar}{{K^*}}
\newcommand{\Kempirical}{{K^*}}
\newcommand{\Ktheory}{{K^*_{\text{theory}}}}
\newcommand{\auc}{\text{AUC}}
\newcommand{\jsd}{\text{JSD}}
\newcommand{\tpm}{\ensuremath{\,\pm\,}}

\title{Generational Depth Estimation of Recursive Synthetic Text:\\Measuring the Discrimination Boundary}

\author{Anonymous}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

% === Abstract ===
\begin{abstract}
Web-scale corpora are increasingly contaminated by recursive synthetic content, yet no method estimates a document's generational depth.
We measure the discrimination boundary $\Kempirical$ under prompt-chain generation using 15 model-free statistical features and an OBD-LightGBM classifier.
Under these features, $\Kempirical{=}2$ for web text at $\theta{=}0.60$ (stable across $\theta \in [0.575, 0.625]$; 8/9 configurations across five families, 1B--14B), $\Kempirical{=}1$--$2$ for code, and $\Kempirical{=}3$ for arXiv.
A fine-tuned DeBERTa-v3-base classifier cannot break $\Kempirical{=}2$ on web text but extends it to 3 on Wikipedia and 4 on arXiv, establishing a two-tier ceiling: model-free features set a domain-dependent floor while neural classifiers recover additional depth signal on structurally rich domains.
A single generation pass renders synthetic web text statistically indistinguishable from deeper generations (\emph{one-wash laundering}).
Largely generator-independent features enable 80--92\% accuracy retention at $\leq$1.5B and 96--100\% mean AUC retention at 7B across tested continuation-mode generators, though boundary-level transfer remains fragile; cross-scorer validation confirms $\Kempirical$ robustness for non-borderline configurations (20/22).
As a consistency check, $\Kempirical{=}2$ correctly predicts binary $>$ graduated filtering for web text, though with limited effect sizes (0.76\,pp MMLU).
\end{abstract}

% === Section 1: Introduction ===
\input{introduction}

% === Section 2: Related Work ===
\input{related_work}

% === Section 3: Method ===
\input{method}

% === Section 4: Experiments ===
\input{experiments_c1}

% === Section 5: K*-Aware Data Filtering ===
\input{experiments_c3}

% === Section 6: Discussion ===
\input{discussion}

% === Section 7: Conclusion ===
\input{conclusion}

% === Limitations (ACL required, unnumbered) ===
\section*{Limitations}

Our findings are bounded by:
(1)~\emph{Scope}: full 9-configuration characterization at $\leq$14B (5 architecture families), with 32B-scale validation via LightGBM and DeBERTa (Section~\ref{sec:neural_probe}); English text (C4, Wikipedia, arXiv) and Python code (CodeSearchNet), four decoding strategies; arXiv $\Kempirical{=}3$ is confirmed with five models across four architecture families but with reduced sample size ($n{=}2{,}200$ per model); code domain tested with five models across four families (Qwen2.5-1.5B/7B, LLaMA-3.1-8B, Mistral-7B-v0.3, $\Kempirical{=}2$; Pythia-1.4B, $\Kempirical{=}1$ with 1v2 CI upper bound $> \theta$); subsampling analysis indicates $n{\geq}2{,}000$ suffices for 100\% reliable $\Kempirical$ determination (Appendix~\ref{sec:power_curve}).
The 1.5B$\to$32B trend shows stable $\Kempirical{=}2$ with declining 1v2 AUC; whether this holds at 70B+ scales remains unverified.
(2)~\emph{Protocol}: all main experiments use prompt-chain, which isolates text-intrinsic degradation from training dynamics; real-world contamination involves both prompting and retraining, and retrain-chain yields $\Kempirical{=}7$ (both Qwen-1.5B and Pythia-1.4B LoRA; Appendix~\ref{sec:extended_limitations}), so prompt-chain $\Kempirical$ may underestimate discriminability in mixed settings.
(3)~\emph{Threshold}: $\Kempirical$ ranges from 2--3 at $\theta{=}0.55$ to 1 at $\theta{=}0.70$; 8/9 configurations yield $\Kempirical{=}2$ at $\theta{=}0.60$ (Mistral-7B: $\Kempirical{=}1$).
(4)~\emph{Marginal boundary}: Qwen-continuation's 1v2 CI lower bound exceeds $\theta$ by only 0.002; cross-scorer validation drops this to $\Kempirical{=}1$ (Section~\ref{sec:transfer}). LLaMA-3.1-8B (C4) and Mistral-7B-v0.3 (code) represent the most marginal $\Kempirical{=}2$ assignments, with 1v2 CI lower bounds within 0.3 percentage points of $\theta$.
(5)~\emph{Feature set}: the 15D features yield a feature-set-specific lower bound ($\Ktheory$ may be strictly higher); $K^*_{\text{neural}}$ is model-dependent ($3$ for Qwen, $2$ for Pythia/OLMo).
(6)~\emph{Segment length}: $\Kempirical{=}2$ confirmed for 128 and 256 tokens (CI lower $> \theta$); 512 tokens yields point estimate $\Kempirical{=}2$ but CI lower marginally below $\theta$ ($0.5979$); the boundary weakens at longer generation lengths.
(7)~\emph{Curation applicability}: the filtering evaluation is validated on two architectures (Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct and Pythia-1.4B) with consistent strategy ordering (Appendix~\ref{sec:pythia_filtering}), though absolute improvement remains small and effect sizes diminish at lower contamination ratios (10--50\%; Appendix~\ref{sec:dose_response}).
(8)~\emph{Family balance}: the Qwen2.5 family provides 5/9 configurations; though spanning three scales (1.5B, 7B, 14B) and two training variants (base, instruct), aggregate statistics weight this family more heavily than the four single-configuration families.
(9)~\emph{Transfer coverage}: cross-model transfer validation shows 80--92\% accuracy retention at $\leq$1.5B (4 configurations) and 96--100\% mean pairwise AUC retention at 7B (3 models, 3 architecture families; Appendix~\ref{sec:7b_transfer}); however, at the $\Kempirical$ boundary (1v2), 4/6 off-diagonal 7B pairs fall below $\theta$, indicating that boundary-level transfer remains fragile even as aggregate transfer strengthens with scale.
Additional limitations (cross-model scoring sensitivity, feature ranking variability at the 1v2 boundary, structural composition confound analysis) appear in Appendix~\ref{sec:extended_limitations}.

% === Ethics Statement (ACL required, unnumbered) ===
\section*{Ethics Statement}

Generational depth estimation is intended for data curation and corpus auditing, positive-sum applications that improve training data quality.
Our experiments use publicly available corpora (C4, Wikipedia, arXiv abstracts) and do not involve personally identifiable information.
A potential dual-use concern is that depth estimation techniques could inform adversarial strategies to evade detection (e.g., by calibrating rewriting to mimic earlier-generation statistics); we note that this risk is shared by all detection research and that the binary detection boundary ($\auc \geq 0.96$ at 0v1) remains robust.
Code and generated corpora will be released upon acceptance to enable reproducibility.

\bibliography{references}

\input{appendix}
\input{appendix_extended}

\end{document}

\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

Under prompt-chain recursion, $\Kempirical{=}2$ for web text at $\theta{=}0.60$ (8/9 configurations; stable across $\theta \in [0.575, 0.625]$), $\Kempirical{=}1$--$2$ for code, and $\Kempirical{=}3$ for arXiv; retrain-chain extends to $\Kempirical{=}7$ (Appendix~\ref{sec:extended_limitations}).
The domain ordering (arXiv $>$ C4 $\geq$ Code $>$ Wikipedia) is $\theta$-invariant.
A fine-tuned DeBERTa classifier cannot break $\Kempirical{=}2$ on C4 (1.5B, 14B, 32B) but extends it to 3 on Wikipedia and 4 on arXiv, establishing a two-tier ceiling where the classifier gap scales with domain structural complexity; future work includes retrain-chain measurement and trends beyond 32B.

\section{Related Work}
\label{sec:related_work}

\paragraph{Model collapse and recursive degradation.}
Iterative training on model outputs causes tail-distribution degradation~\cite{shumailov2024collapse, alemohammad2024selfconsuming, dohmatob2024model, dohmatob2025strong}, diversity contraction~\cite{guo2024curious, padmakumar2024writing, briesch2023selfconsuming}, and predictable convergence~\cite{bertrand2024stability, schaeffer2025position, kazdan2025collapse, gerstgrasser2024model, shi2025collapse}.
\citet{gambetta2024surplexity} propose surplexity, a single surprisal-based collapse indicator; our 15-dimensional feature set captures richer distributional structure and enables cross-model transfer.
\citet{amin2025escaping} show that weak-data curation can mitigate collapse, connecting to our filtering evaluation (Section~\ref{sec:filtering}).
Our prompt-chain operates in the replace regime~\cite{kazdan2025collapse}; $\Kempirical = 2$ is therefore a conservative lower bound.
This literature establishes convergence but provides no per-text depth measurement, does not predict domain dependence of saturation (Wikipedia depth 1 vs.\ arXiv depth 3), and does not characterize the prompt-chain vs.\ retrain-chain gap ($\Kempirical{=}2$ vs.\ $7$).

\paragraph{Beyond binary detection.}
Binary detectors~\cite{gehrmann2019gltr, mitchell2023detectgpt, bao2024fast, yang2024dnagpt, hans2024binoculars, verma2024ghostbuster, basani2026diveye, zhang2024minkpp} achieve strong human-vs.-machine separation but produce no depth estimate; surveys~\cite{wu2023survey, tang2024science} consolidate this landscape but frame detection as binary.
Recent work extends beyond binary labels to collaboration degree~\cite{cheng2025beyond}, contamination hierarchies~\cite{mehta2025beyond}, and multigenerator benchmarks~\cite{wang2024semeval}; robustness concerns arise under paraphrasing~\cite{sadasivan2023detect, krishna2023paraphrasing} and cross-mode transfer~\cite{hao2025rewrite}; CoDeC~\cite{zawalski2026codec} addresses contamination via membership inference.
None estimates recursive generation depth.
Watermarking~\cite{kirchenbauer2023watermark} embeds detectable signals at generation time but does not apply to text already in the wild.

\paragraph{Distributional quality metrics.}
MAUVE~\cite{pillutla2021mauve, pillutla2023mauve} measures distributional closeness between generated and reference text at the population level; nucleus sampling~\cite{holtzman2020curious} addresses degeneration but not multi-generational degradation.
These metrics complement our per-text approach: MAUVE captures corpus-level quality shifts, while $\Kempirical$ localizes the generation at which individual texts become indistinguishable from deeper outputs.

\paragraph{Data selection and curation.}
DSIR~\cite{xie2023dsir}, D4~\cite{tirumala2023d4}, DoReMi~\cite{xie2023doremi}, and FineWeb~\cite{penedo2024fineweb} address corpus quality for large web corpora such as C4~\cite{raffel2020t5} through domain-aware filtering and deduplication but are depth-agnostic; Drayson et al.~\cite{drayson2025} combined binary detection with importance resampling, a special case when $\Kstar = 1$.
\citet{oren2024proving} address contamination detection in pre-training data; our method extends this to depth-granular resolution.

\paragraph{Ordinal classification and probing.}
Our surprisal features build on information-theoretic processing cost~\cite{hale2001surprisal, levy2008expectation}, repurposed to track generational degradation.
Our OBD classifier follows Frank \& Hall's~\cite{frank2001ordinal} binary decomposition; CORAL~\cite{cao2020coral} provides a rank-consistent neural alternative.
Our neural probe extends probing~\cite{alain2017understanding, belinkov2022probing} to the discrimination boundary.

\section{Experiments}
\label{sec:experiments}

We evaluate generational depth estimation on nine model configurations spanning five architecture families:
Pythia-1.4B, OLMo-1B, Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct (rewrite and continuation), Qwen2.5-1.5B (base), Qwen2.5-7B, Qwen2.5-14B, LLaMA-3.1-8B, and Mistral-7B, all in continuation mode except where noted.
All experiments use the OBD-LightGBM classifier with the 15-dimensional feature set (Section~\ref{sec:method}), with 5{,}000 texts per depth level (generations 0--5; power analysis in Appendix~\ref{sec:power_curve}).

%% ====================================================================
\subsection{\texorpdfstring{$\Kempirical \leq 2$}{K*<=2} Across Model Configurations (C4)}
\label{sec:kstar_boundary}

\begin{table*}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Pairwise $\auc$ for adjacent depth pairs with bootstrap 95\% CIs (1{,}000 resamples) and permutation $p$-values.
$\Kempirical = 2$ requires 1v2 CI lower $> \theta{=}0.60$ and 2v3 CI lower $\leq \theta$ (Eq.~\ref{eq:kstar}).
$\leq$1.5B models use self-scoring; 7B/14B use Qwen-1.5B scorer ($^\ddagger$).
Extended results in Table~\ref{tab:kstar_ci_extended}.}
\label{tab:kstar_ci}
\begin{tabular}{lcccccccccc}
\toprule
 & \multicolumn{2}{c}{Qwen-rw} & \multicolumn{2}{c}{Pythia} & \multicolumn{2}{c}{OLMo} & \multicolumn{2}{c}{Qwen-cont} & \multicolumn{2}{c}{Qwen-7B\rlap{$^\ddagger$}} \\
\cmidrule(lr){2-3}\cmidrule(lr){4-5}\cmidrule(lr){6-7}\cmidrule(lr){8-9}\cmidrule(lr){10-11}
 & $\auc$ & 95\% CI & $\auc$ & 95\% CI & $\auc$ & 95\% CI & $\auc$ & 95\% CI & $\auc$ & 95\% CI \\
\midrule
0v1 & .9956 & & .9932 & & .9970 & & .9971 & & .9638 & [.961,\,.967] \\
1v2 & .6955 & [.686,\,.704]\rlap{$^*$} & .6539 & [.645,\,.663]\rlap{$^*$} & .6145 & [.605,\,.625]\rlap{$^*$} & .6112 & [.602,\,.621]\rlap{$^*$} & .6168 & [.608,\,.626]\rlap{$^*$} \\
2v3 & .5893 & [.580,\,.599] & .5514 & [.542,\,.563] & .5496 & [.540,\,.560] & .5609 & [.551,\,.571] & .5492 & [.539,\,.559] \\
3v4 & .5435 & & .5152 & & .5091 & & .5409 & & .5276 & \\
4v5 & .5340 & & .5136 & & .5045 & & .5141 & & .5085 & \\
\bottomrule
\multicolumn{11}{l}{\footnotesize $^*$Permutation $p < 0.001$ for all 1v2 comparisons shown.} \\
\multicolumn{11}{l}{\footnotesize $^\ddagger$Generator: Qwen2.5-7B (fp16); scorer: Qwen2.5-1.5B.}
\end{tabular}
\end{table*}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig2_auc_decay.pdf}
\caption{Pairwise $\auc$ decay across depth pairs for model-mode configurations (solid) and decoding strategies (dashed). The horizontal dashed line marks $\theta = 0.60$.}
\label{fig:auc_decay}
\end{figure}

Table~\ref{tab:kstar_ci} and Figure~\ref{fig:auc_decay} show $\Kempirical{=}2$ across all five tested architecture families on C4, with 8/9 individual configurations (1v2 $\auc$ range: 0.6112--0.6955; all CI lower bounds $> \theta$; permutation $p < 0.001$); Qwen contributes 5 configurations spanning three scales (1.5B, 7B, 14B).
Mistral-7B is the sole exception ($\Kempirical{=}1$; 1v2 $\auc = 0.594$; Section~\ref{sec:boundary_cases}).
LLaMA-3.1-8B yields $\Kempirical{=}2$ (1v2 $\auc = 0.6123$, CI $[0.603, 0.622]$; borderline: 1/5 folds below $\theta$; Table~\ref{tab:kstar_ci_extended}).
Among non-Qwen families: Pythia and OLMo yield clear $\Kempirical{=}2$, LLaMA is borderline (CI lower $= 0.603$), and Mistral yields $\Kempirical{=}1$.
An independent F1@0.50 analysis confirms the same boundary for all configurations (Appendix~\ref{sec:f1_validation}).
All four decoding strategies likewise yield $\Kempirical{=}2$ (Table~\ref{tab:s2_decoding}).
The 1v2 spread due to generation mode ($\Delta = 8.4$\,pp within Qwen) exceeds the spread across models in continuation mode ($\Delta = 3.9$\,pp).

\paragraph{Scale and instruction-tuning invariance.}
$\Kempirical{=}2$ persists from 1.5B through 14B: Qwen-14B (1v2 $\auc = 0.621$ $[0.612, 0.630]$), Qwen-7B ($0.6168$ $[0.608, 0.626]$), and LLaMA-3.1-8B ($0.6123$) all exceed $\theta$, despite progressively reduced 0v1 signal (0.960 at 14B, 0.964 at 7B, 0.923 at 8B vs.\ $> 0.99$ at $\leq$1.5B).
A base-vs.-instruct ablation at 1.5B likewise yields $\Kempirical = 2$ (1v2 $\auc = 0.626$); the boundary is not an instruction-tuning artifact.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig_kstar_theta.pdf}
\caption{$\Kempirical(\theta)$ sensitivity curve for six configurations. $\Kempirical$: maximum discriminable depth; $\theta$: AUC decision threshold. All configurations yield $\Kempirical{=}2$ at $\theta{=}0.60$ (dashed line). At $\theta{=}0.70$, all drop to $\Kempirical{=}1$.}
\label{fig:kstar_theta}
\end{figure}

Figure~\ref{fig:kstar_theta} shows $\Kempirical$ as a function of $\theta$: web text $\Kempirical{=}2$ is stable across a 5\,pp window ($\theta \in [0.575, 0.625]$), with a gradual transition to $\Kempirical{=}3$ at $\theta{=}0.55$ (two Qwen configurations) and $\Kempirical{=}1$ at $\theta{=}0.70$.
The domain ordering (arXiv $>$ web $\geq$ cross-scorer) is preserved across the full $\theta$ range (Table~\ref{tab:theta_sensitivity}; Figure~\ref{fig:theta_sensitivity} in Appendix); this ordering invariance provides a more robust characterization than absolute $\Kempirical$ values, which shift with $\theta$.

%% ====================================================================
\subsection{Largely Generator-Independent Transfer}
\label{sec:transfer}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.85\columnwidth]{figures/fig3_transfer_heatmap.pdf}
\caption{Cross-model transfer heatmap. Each cell shows 6-class ordinal accuracy when trained on the row model and tested on the column model, divided by in-domain accuracy (retention rate). Pythia$\leftrightarrow$OLMo: 90--92\%; Qwen-rewrite$\to$continuation: 61--63\%.}
\label{fig:transfer_heatmap}
\end{figure}

Aggregate transfer is strong: continuation-mode classifiers retain 80--92\% ordinal accuracy across generators at $\leq$1.5B (Figure~\ref{fig:transfer_heatmap}; Table~\ref{tab:s1_transfer}), and 96--100\% mean pairwise AUC at 7B (Appendix~\ref{sec:7b_transfer}).
However, boundary-level transfer is fragile: at 7B, 4/6 off-diagonal 1v2 pairs fall below $\theta$, so $\Kempirical{=}2$ assignments do not transfer at the boundary even as overall discriminability improves with scale.
Qwen-rewrite classifiers transfer poorly (61--63\%), consistent with reliance on mode-specific artifacts.

\paragraph{Cross-scorer validation.}
Replacing the self-scorer with Pythia-1.4B or OLMo-1B preserves feature rankings ($\rho > 0.90$; Table~\ref{tab:crossscorer}).
$\Kempirical$ is robust for non-borderline configurations: across 22 cross-scorer configurations on C4 and arXiv, $\Kempirical$ was preserved in 20; the two exceptions (Qwen-cont scored by Pythia on C4, Qwen-7B cross-scored on arXiv) are the most marginal boundary configurations, with AUC within 0.002 of $\theta$.
Qwen-cont is the most scorer-sensitive continuation-mode configuration: its self-scored 1v2 CI lower bound exceeds $\theta$ by only 0.2\,pp (Table~\ref{tab:kstar_ci}), and cross-family scoring pushes it below $\theta$.
For arXiv, Pythia-1.4B cross-scoring preserves $\Kempirical{=}3$ for 3/4 generators (2v3 $\auc$: 0.608--0.632); Qwen-7B drops to borderline $\Kempirical{=}2$ ($\Delta = -2.7$\,pp), consistent with Qwen scorer sensitivity on C4 and Wikipedia (Table~\ref{tab:arxiv_crossscorer_ci}).
A three-way ANOVA (Generator $\times$ Scorer $\times$ Depth-Pair; $N{=}300$; Table~\ref{tab:anova_crossscorer}) confirms that Depth-Pair dominates variance ($\eta^2_p = 0.998$); Scorer is statistically significant ($p < .001$) but practically minor ($\eta^2_p = 0.192$), consistent with a detectable but small scorer influence on absolute discriminability.
F1 at a balanced threshold ($t{=}0.50$) independently confirms scorer independence: across 12 cross-scorer configurations, F1 shifts are below 0.03 and the 1v2$\to$2v3 boundary pattern is preserved (Appendix~\ref{sec:f1_validation}).

%% ====================================================================
\subsection{Feature Ordering and Generation Mode}
\label{sec:feature_degradation}
\label{sec:generation_mode}

Among continuation-mode models, single-feature $\auc$ rankings are highly correlated ($\rho \geq 0.85$; Table~\ref{tab:top_features}).
The top-3 features (\texttt{surp\_d1\_std}, \texttt{surp\_std}, \texttt{surp\_d2\_std}) are shared across all three families: autoregressive generation progressively damps surprisal variability; however, no single feature subset of size ${<}8$ reliably sustains $\Kempirical{=}2$ (Appendix~\ref{sec:feature_subset}).
A feature group ablation (Appendix~\ref{sec:feature_ablation}) confirms that surprisal (base) dominates on arXiv (single AUC $= 0.809$, LOO $\Delta = 0.031$); on C4 and Wikipedia, all LOO drops fall within fold variance ($\leq 0.007$), indicating the boundary signal is carried by feature combinations rather than any single indicator.
Leave-one-feature-out analysis identifies \texttt{freq\_entropy} as the most stable contributor across all three domains (Appendix~\ref{sec:feature_ablation}).
Qwen-rewrite diverges (\texttt{freq\_entropy} dominates), consistent with its poor cross-model transfer (61--63\%).
Within Qwen, rewrite yields 1v2 $\auc = 0.6955$ vs.\ continuation's $0.6112$ ($\Delta = +8.4$\,pp); continuation mode is therefore recommended as the canonical condition.

%% ====================================================================
\subsection{Neural Probe}
\label{sec:neural_probe}

Replacing the 15D features with frozen encoder hidden states (MLP probe, 5{,}000 texts per depth; Table~\ref{tab:neural_probe_crossmodel}), Qwen achieves $K^*_{\text{neural}} = 3$ (2v3 $\auc = 0.6159$), while Pythia and OLMo remain at $K^*_{\text{neural}} = 2$ ($\auc_{2v3}$: 0.5963, 0.5892).
The Qwen-only elevation reflects self-familiarity artifacts that transfer poorly (Section~\ref{sec:transfer}); the 15D statistical features yield consistent $\Kempirical = 2$ across all families.

